WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.760
Hey everyone, welcome to Models and Agents Episode 104 on July 7th, 2026.

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:07.660
Let's see what shipped.

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:09.480
The AI world never sleeps.

00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:11.340
Here's what you need to know today.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:15.300
Real-time voice agents just got cheaper and more capable.

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:21.020
Open AI split its real-time API into specialized models with lower latency.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:25.200
Open AI released two new models for voice agents today,

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:31.820
while 10cent dropped a large, open mixture of experts model and NVIDIA added audio support

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:33.780
to one of its NemoTron variants.

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:39.380
And Thropic also shared new interpretability tools that run on open-weight models.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:45.340
On the agent side, a new local coding harness appeared and community experiments highlighted

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:48.500
both quick training wins and quantization pitfalls.

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:54.200
The top story today centers on open AI's updates to its real-time voice stack.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:04.700
Open AI added GPT real-time, 2.1 and GPT real-time, 2.1 times mini to its API for low latency voice agents.

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:12.160
The mini variant functions as a reasoning model and carries the same pricing as the prior GPT real-time mini.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:18.660
Both models use improved caching that cuts P95 latency by at least 25%.

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:24.780
The updates target agent voice use cases and include web RTC connection support.

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:30.560
Developers can now run more responsive voice loops without moving to higher cost tiers.

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:37.600
This release continues the ongoing work on reliable agent tool calling that we saw discussed last week.

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:43.340
Watch for how the split models affect tool calling stability in production voice agents.

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:50.420
Tencent released high-three and opened 295 billion-parameter mixture of experts model.

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:58.620
The model activates 21 billion-parameters per token and carries a 256,000-token context window.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:05.200
It is aimed at reasoning and agent tasks and report 78.0 on skim bench,

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:07.900
verified along with lower hallucination rates.

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:15.660
High-three is released under Apache 2.0 and remains free to try on open router through July 21st.

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:21.180
This moves the open-weight performance conversation forward from the updates covered yesterday

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:26.260
by showing another Chinese lab closing the gap on long-context agent workloads.

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:37.380
Nvidia released Nemotron Labs-Odex-30B-A3B, a unified audio text model built on the 30 billion

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:41.340
parameter MoE, Nemotron Cascade 2 backbone.

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:48.340
The model uses three billion active parameters, adds discrete audio tokens and an audio encoder

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:51.760
and keeps text reasoning and agent performance intact.

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:57.340
It supports thinking and instruct modes plus a 1 million token context window.

00:02:58.060 --> 00:03:03.860
Teams working on speech-to-speech or audio augmented agents can pull the checkpoint from hugging face

00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:07.000
for direct comparison against the text-only version.

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:13.380
Anthropic partnered with Neuronpedia to release an interactive demo of its interpretability methods

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:18.560
on open-weight models. The demo is available at Neuronpedia.org,

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:24.660
slash JLens, and accompanies a paper on how Claude develops mechanisms for conscious access.

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:29.740
Researchers can now inspect similar circuits in models they host themselves.

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:37.300
Coder is a new goal-based coding and computer-use harness released after 1,300 commits.

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:45.880
It targets local Linux setups with llama.cpp and models such as QN3.627bq8.

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:53.200
The tool supports skills, MCP, visual models, milestone planning, multi-chat orchestration,

00:03:53.200 --> 00:04:00.260
and an embedded file browser. A single binary runs offline or with any open AI compatible endpoint.

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:05.540
Users report strong results on reverse engineering and open-escat loops.

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:11.760
This adds another concrete option to the agent tooling layer we discussed in yesterday's episode.

00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:20.220
A 117 million-parameter Cilia architecture model was trained from scratch on an H-100 in five hours.

00:04:21.020 --> 00:04:25.660
Training used the synth 100m dataset and the muon optimizer.

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:32.320
The model and code are available on hugging face and github, along with an inference script that runs

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:39.340
via UV. The model remains severely under-trained on only 82 million tokens, so further scaling

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:49.780
experiments are expected from the community. Users report that QN3.627b in NVFP4 and FP8 quantization

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:58.840
on Blackwell hardware with VLLM 0.24.0 shows mid-task halting and repetitive failure loops in thinking

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:07.880
mode. The BF-16 version runs reliably, and the issues appear less frequent in FP8 than in NVFP4.

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:15.440
Teams running agentic workloads should benchmark quantized checkpoints against BF-16 before deploying.

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:23.700
Tried GPT real-time Ride History 2.1 Mini through the open AI API for voice agent prototypes.

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:28.380
The lower latency and new pricing make longer sessions practical.

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:36.300
Pull 10 cents high three from open router before July 21st, and run it on school bench style tasks

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:44.460
to compare the 21 billion active parameters against QN3.627. Test coder on a local Linux machine

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:50.100
with your preferred coding model. Its milestone planner and file browser integration are worth

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:57.280
evaluating for offline agent loops. Run the Neuronpedia JSpace demo on an open-weight model you

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:02.320
already host to inspect attention patterns without needing anthropic infrastructure.

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:10.240
More quantized checkpoints for QN3.627b are expected as Blackwell adoption grows.

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:18.120
Additional open MoE releases from Chinese labs are likely following high three's Apache 2.0 move.

00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:25.560
Watch for VLLM updates addressing the NVFP4 agent loop instability reported this week.

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:32.500
Okay, let's pop the hood on mixture of experts routing. Everyone talks about MoE as a simple

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:38.900
activate fewer parameters get free speed trick. In practice, the router must decide which experts

00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:44.140
to wake for every token, and that decision sits at the center of the efficiency story.

00:06:44.620 --> 00:06:49.400
A lightweight router network scores every expert and typically keeps the top K.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:56.420
The cost of that scoring grows linearly with total expert count, so labs add auxiliary load balancing

00:06:56.420 --> 00:07:02.420
losses during training to stop the router from always picking the same few experts. When the router is

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:10.380
well tuned, inference only loads the active experts into memory, which is why a 295 billion MoE can run

00:07:10.380 --> 00:07:16.100
at roughly the speed of a 30 billion dense model. The hidden cost appears at training time.

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:22.840
Every token still computes router logits across all experts. Poor routing can leave some experts

00:07:22.840 --> 00:07:29.360
under trained creating quality cliffs on out-of-domain data. The practical rule of thumb is to watch

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:36.060
expert utilization histograms. If any expert sits below one to two percent activation, you are wasting

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:41.380
parameters and should either increase the load balancing coefficient or reduce total experts.

00:07:42.220 --> 00:07:47.960
Teams choosing between dense and MoE should therefore measure both tokens per second at inference

00:07:47.960 --> 00:07:53.820
and the variance in expert activation during their target workload, rather than trusting headline

00:07:53.820 --> 00:08:02.480
parameter counts. Before we go, keep an eye on VLLM updates addressing the NVFP4 instability and

00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:09.320
the next round of open MoE releases expected after high three. That wraps up today's AI briefing.

